'''Balistidæ''' (ba-lis′ti-dē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Balistes'' + ''-idæ''.] A family of fishes, typified by the genus ''Balistes'', adopted by different authors with various limits. {{smaller|(''a'') In Bonaparte’s early system, 1832, a family embracing the ''Balistidæ'', ''Triacanthidæ'', and ''Ostraciontidæ'', and thus equivalent to the ''Sclerodermes'' of Cuvier. (''b'') In Bonaparte’s later systems (1840, etc.), a family embracing the ''Balistidæ'' and ''Triacanthidæ'', thus equivalent to the suborder ''Sclerodermi'' of Gill. (''c'') In Swainson’s system, a family including all the plectognath fishes. (''d'') In Gill’s system, a family of scleroderm plectognaths with reduced rhombiform or more or less spiniform dermal appendages; a compressed body; teeth few in number and more or less compressed; a long pelvis, compressed and arcuate, with the tip sometimes prominent and sometimes concealed; and no paired ventral fins or spines. The species are numerous in tropical and subtropical seas, and are divided into three subfamilies, the ''Balistinæ'', ''Monacanthinæ'', and ''Psilocephalinæ''. See these words. Species are known as ''trigger-fish'', ''file-fish'', etc.}}

'''Balistina''' (bal-is-tī′nä̤), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Balistes'' + ''-ina''.] In Günther’s classification of fishes, the second group of his family ''Sclerodermi'', identical with the family ''Balistidæ'' of recent authors.

'''Balistinæ''' (bal-is-tī′nē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Balistes'' + ''-inæ''.] '''1.''' A subfamily of balistoid fishes having few vertebræ (17), an anterior dorsal fin consisting of 3 (rarely 2) spines, of which the first is enlarged and the second locks it in erection, branchial apertures behind the eyes, a compressed ovate form, and rhombiform scales. {{smaller|The most common English names of the species are ''file-fish'' and ''trigger-fish''. The flesh is generally but little esteemed, and may even be poisonous; but in some places, as in Bermuda, one of the species of the genus ''Balistes'' is highly esteemed and locally called ''turbot''. The skin is used for filing and as a substitute for sandpaper. See cut under ''Balistes''.}}

