land unplowed, < ''balk'', a ridge: see ''balk''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.'' Cf. Norw. ''balka'', do clumsy work.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1†.''' To make a balk or ridge in plowing; make a ridge in by leaving a strip unplowed.
{{smaller block|<poem>To tille a felde man must have diligence,
And ''balk'' it not.</poem>
{{right|''Palladius'', Husbondrie (E. E. T. S.), p. 8.}}}}
Hence —'''2†.''' To leave untouched generally; omit; pass over; neglect; shun.
{{smaller block|''Balk'' logic with acquaintance that you have.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of the S., i. 1.}}
By reason of y<sup>e</sup> contagion then in London, we ''baulked'' the inns.
{{right|''Evelyn'', Diary, Oct. 10, 1641.}}}}
'''3.''' To place a balk in the way of; hence, to hinder; thwart; frustrate; disappoint.
{{smaller block|My Sport is always ''balkt'', or cut short — I stumble over the game I would pursue.
{{right|''Congreve'', Old Batchelor, iv. 5.}}
Alike to the citizen and to the legislator, home experiences daily supply proofs that the conduct of human beings ''balks'' calculation.
{{right|''H. Spencer'', Sins of Legislators, ii.}}}}
'''4†.''' To miss by error or inadvertence.
{{smaller block|You cannot ''baulk'' your Road without the hazard of drowning.
{{right|''Feltham'', Low Countries (1677), p. 46. (''N. E. D.'')}}}}
'''5†.''' To heap up so as to form a balk or ridge. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Ten thousand bold Scots, two-and-twenty knights,
''Balk’d'' in their own blood, did Sir Walter see
On Holmedon’s plains.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. IV., i. 1.}}}}
[Some editors read ''bak’d'' in this passage.] ='''Syn. 3.''' {{smaller|''Foil'', ''Thwart'', etc. See ''frustrate''.}}

